WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
A cell looks like one bag.

2
00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:04.380
But where does all its volume go?

3
00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.260
Chapter one followed amount over volume as a yeast cell grew.

4
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.700
Now ask what share each compartment takes, and whether that share holds during

5
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:15.700
growth.

6
00:00:18.567 --> 00:00:22.767
A membrane-bound compartment is a region enclosed by a membrane.

7
00:00:23.987 --> 00:00:27.267
The nucleus, which holds most of the DNA, is one.

8
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:31.327
Divide its volume by the cell's, and you get its share.

9
00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:37.947
Here is one carefully measured cell line: hamster V79, growing in a dish.

10
00:00:39.107 --> 00:00:42.247
The nucleus fills about 27% % of the cell.

11
00:00:43.727 --> 00:00:47.087
Mitochondria, another compartment, fill about 5%.

12
00:00:48.067 --> 00:00:51.327
Now a fission yeast, a rod that grows at its ends.

13
00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:54.187
Its nucleus is about 8%.

14
00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:59.947
percent. In this yeast, the nucleus grows with the cell, so its share holds as the

15
00:00:59.947 --> 00:01:00.947
cell grows.

16
00:01:01.667 --> 00:01:04.767
Two careful measurements, two very different answers.

17
00:01:06.107 --> 00:01:10.527
So which number is the nuclear share of a cell: 27%, % or 8%?

18
00:01:11.807 --> 00:01:12.827
Pause and decide.

19
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.630
Both, each for its own cell.

20
00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.170
There is no universal nuclear fraction.

21
00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:30.010
A share means something only with its cell type attached, so carry the cell type

22
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.010
with every number.

23
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.650
One question stays open.

24
00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:38.930
Does the nucleus follow the DNA inside it, or the cell around it?

25
00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:42.550
The design problem below lets you test it.

26
00:01:46.417 --> 00:01:48.977
Plant cells change their shares as they grow.

27
00:01:50.257 --> 00:01:54.417
Take the outer layer of a root in Arabidopsis, a small lab plant.

28
00:01:55.657 --> 00:01:59.077
In young cells, the vacuole fills about 30 to 40%.

29
00:01:59.077 --> 00:02:00.077
percent.

30
00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:06.120
As the cells lengthen, vacuolar occupancy rises to about 80 to 90%.

31
00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.720
percent. Across the comparison, cell volume is about 14-fold greater.

32
00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:17.040
But space outside the vacuole is only about two- to threefold greater.

33
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:21.100
Most of the added cell volume is vacuolar.

34
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:27.440
The smaller rise outside it requires relatively little new RNA and protein.

35
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.500
This can ease a biosynthetic cost of being big.

36
00:02:35.467 --> 00:02:37.027
Which cells have which parts?

37
00:02:38.327 --> 00:02:44.367
Animal, plant and fungal cells share a plasma membrane, nucleus, mitochondria, ER,

38
00:02:44.807 --> 00:02:45.967
Golgi, and cytoskeleton.

39
00:02:47.307 --> 00:02:52.387
For these core parts, focus on three differences: walls, plastids, and vacuoles.

40
00:02:53.647 --> 00:02:57.867
A root cell deep in soil usually has plastids, but no chloroplasts.

41
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:02.007
Fission yeast keeps its nuclear share as it grows.

42
00:03:03.607 --> 00:03:08.987
Shares are cell-specific; elongating Arabidopsis root cells add mostly vacuolar

43
00:03:08.987 --> 00:03:09.487
volume.
